       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, because the dose is unsafe as written. Acetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6 g/day. The usual maximum for a healthy adult is 4 g/day, and many institutions cap it at 3 g/day. A single 2000 mg dose is also above the standard maximum of 1000 mg per dose. Repeated doses at this level risk hepatotoxicity, even with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some common regimens for a 70 kg adult with normal hepatic and renal function:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3 g/day), which is conservative and often preferred.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily** (4 g/day), which is the maximum. Doses should be at least 6 hours apart.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2.6 g/day).\n\nPossible explanations for the 2000 mg figure are a typo, such as 1000 mg or 500 mg, or a confusion between the per-dose and daily amounts. Please check the intended dose with your resident or attending. Tell me which regimen you want and I'll enter it right away. If 2000 mg three times daily really was intended, it needs to be confirmed with the supervising prescriber first."
      }
